@@ -0,0 +1,35 @@
+---
+id: dl-005-rentals-of-three-days-or-more-get-10-percent-off-rounded-to-the-cent
+type: decision-log
+title: "Rentals of three days or more get 10 percent off, rounded to the cent"
+status: pending
+format: 1
+---
+
+# Context
+
+The school wants to reward customers who stay longer. The brief says a rental of 3 days or more costs
+10% less than its full price, and that quotes and bookings both show the reduced total.
+
+dl-002 fixes the price as an exact integer number of euro cents. A 10% discount does not always give a
+whole number of cents: 3 days at 2501 cents is 6752.7 cents. The brief does not say how to round it.
+
+# Decision
+
+- A period of 3 days or more is discounted. Its total is the full price, days times `dailyPrice`, less
+  10%. A period of 2 days or fewer is not discounted.
+- The discounted total is rounded to the nearest cent, with halves rounded up. For example 6752.7 becomes
+  6753 and 2713.5 becomes 2714.
+- `quote` and `book` both get the total from the same pricing function, so they always agree.
+- The discount is computed in integer arithmetic, so no floating-point error reaches the rounding.
+- This amends dl-002, whose price rule said the price involves no rounding. That is no longer true for
+  a discounted period. Full-price periods are still exact.
+
+# Consequences
+
+- Customers pay the nearest cent, and a half cent goes to the customer's favour. A customer is never
+  charged more than the exact 90% price plus half a cent.
+- A discounted total is no longer always a multiple of the daily price, so a client must not divide the
+  total by the number of days to recover the daily price.
+- The rule is fixed in code. Changing the percentage, the threshold or the rounding is a change of this
+  decision.
